# tools/debug_drop_sites.lldb — resolve the SITES behind a debug-allocator verdict.
#
# The debug allocator (--debug-alloc / WITH_DEBUG_ALLOC=1) reports WHICH block
# double-freed or leaked (address, size, the drop-origin tag of the first free).
# This file resolves WHERE, with recipes that finish in seconds on real programs.
# (The old version put unconditional breakpoints with backtraces on every
# rt_alloc/rt_free; it did not finish within five minutes on a four-test fixture.)
#
# 0. Classify first, no debugger:
#      WITH_DEBUG_ALLOC=1 ./bin            -> the verdict: block, size, first-drop tag
#      WITH_ALLOC_NO_REUSE=1 ./bin         -> report persists: genuine double free;
#                                             report vanishes: stale pointer into
#                                             reused memory, or an uninitialized
#                                             value holding a live address (#729)
#
# 1. Every alloc and free of ONE address, with drop origins — the allocator's
#    own trap (works without WITH_DEBUG_ALLOC, so the heap layout is unchanged):
#      WITH_DEBUG_ALLOC_TRAP_FREE=<decimal payload addr> ./bin
#    prints each hit; add WITH_DEBUG_ALLOC_TRAP_FREE_HIT=<n> to panic on the
#    n-th free so a debugger stops on that call chain. Values may be zero-padded
#    to keep the environment block the same length across runs (heap replay).
#
# 2. Sites with backtraces, under lldb (ASLR is off under lldb, so an address
#    from run 0 is stable):
#      lldb --batch -s tools/debug_drop_sites.lldb \
#          -o "settings set target.env-vars WITH_DEBUG_ALLOC=1 WITH_DEBUG_ALLOC_TRAP_FREE=<addr> WITH_TEST_FILTER=<test>" \
#          -o run -o "bt 24" -o quit -- ./bin
#    The trap-check breakpoints below fire once per alloc/free of <addr> (with
#    a backtrace each, via their command lists); the reporter breakpoint fires
#    at the second free; the final `bt 24` is the stop that ended the run.
#
#    For a fixture that fails only under `with test`, debug the runner's own
#    artifact — out/<dir>/<stem>.test.<pid>.<nanos> — not a `with build` of the
#    file (#1013 tracks keeping it).
#
# Do NOT set conditional breakpoints (`--condition '$x0 == <addr>'`) on rt_free
# or rt_alloc: the expression is evaluated per hit and the run never finishes.
# A hardware watchpoint on the payload (`watchpoint set expression -w write
# -s 8 -- <addr>`) is the other fast tool when the trap env is not enough.

breakpoint set --name dbg_trap_free_check
breakpoint command add --one-liner "bt 12" --one-liner "continue"

breakpoint set --name dbg_trap_alloc_check
breakpoint command add --one-liner "bt 12" --one-liner "continue"

breakpoint set --name dbg_report_double_free
